title: 'View requests: (viewRequests ...)'
pid: 77243a37-3470-44f1-95fe-f51108460122
body: |2-
  =(viewRequests ...)= carries client→server requests for viewbranches
  (unlike =(view ...)= and the stats below, which are server→client). On save
  the refresh fulfills each request and then drops the atom, so a
  request is transient (the refresh's request rung, in
  =docs/data-model_technical.org=, "The refresh: how a view follows
  the graph"). Request forms:

  In a content view, an occurrence that an editable-view request
  extends -- a fresh view's roots, and the editable content the request
  draws or shows under them -- shows its =forksFolder= where it has a
  fork partner, either way, but no other top-level relation folder,
  unasked. This applies both to ordinary positions and to nodes
  extended as members of relation folders, but for a member as such of
  a =forksFolder=, which shows one only when asked ='(folder forks)'=:
  the folder it stands in lists every mirror of its note already
  (=docs/forks.org=, "Every mirror, flat"). Each forkee as such (a
  claiming member of the =forksFolder= of an editable occurrence) is
  drawn as content is: editable, carrying an editable-view request its
  own visit consumes, unless another occurrence holds its node's
  editability or the node budget is spent; a foreign one too. One the
  buffer already shows write-protected stays so at a plain rerender;
  =(viewRequests editableView)= on its forker extends it. An editable
  forkee as such saves its title and body when the user owns it (a
  foreign one's must match the disk), and a child placed under it that
  its list does not name (typed there, or moved from elsewhere) joins
  its content when the user owns it (the save is refused under a
  foreign one). Each forker as such (a member of that =forksFolder=
  claiming nothing; in a view the server draws, a note that forks the
  occurrence's note and is not forked by it, or an indirect member, a
  mirror of the note joined to it only through others, after them) is
  drawn the same way, showing what it adds to the
  note's content; its title and body, and a child placed under it,
  are read as a forkee as such's, never its content as its whole
  list. A save that cuts one of its children makes the note fork it,
  and hides the child in the note unless the note, or another note the
  user owns, contains it after the save; the saved view gives the forker its claim, so it is redrawn
  among the forkees (a mutual, where it forks the note), without a
  =hiddenByForkerFolder=. Deleting a forker as such hides it in the
  note. Under a foreign note such a save is refused.
  A member as such of either kind shows its content less what the note
  contains or hides and less what another note the user owns contains:
  what the user keeps elsewhere ('docs/forks.org', "What you keep
  elsewhere"). An editable member as such additionally includes its
  nonempty =integratedElsewhereFolder= (what it contains that the user
  keeps elsewhere, write-protected, its members folded, each with a
  =containerFolder= naming where), first; its nonempty
  =hiddenInForkPartnerFolder= after that; an editable forker as such
  that forks the note its nonempty =hiddenByForkerFolder= (what it
  hides of the note's content), after them; a cut from either kind
  hides only what no note the user owns but the member contains after
  the save, and a child the saved buffer shows in either of the first
  two folders is no cut, though the graph changed since the buffer was
  drawn;
  =hiddenOutsideOfForkPartnersFolder=, when nonempty, stands last in
  every =forksFolder= of a content view; emptied by a save, it goes
  from the redrawn view, as from a fresh one. Use a
  folder request for the other relation folders; their potential
  contents remain advertised by the node's heralds. The diff view draws
  no folder unasked but where something changed.

  - =(folder RELNAME)= — build the folders of the relation, populated from the
    graph. RELNAME is =aliases=,
    =hides= (the hider and hidden folders), =forks= (the one
    =forksFolder=, both sides of the relation), =containers=
    or =mentioners=.
    The editable folder (=forksFolder= / =aliasFolder=)
    appears even when empty (its editable "add here" surface); an empty
    write-protected folder is pruned. =containers= and =mentioners= each
    build one write-protected folder (=containerFolder=,
    =mentionerFolder=), pruned when empty: the other side of each is the
    node's content, or its links, shown without a folder. A
    =containerFolder= lists no view-ancestor of its node (a container
    the view already shows above it, however far up); one drawn earlier
    loses it at the next refresh.
    Under each member of a partner folder of any kind but the diff
    view's two acquisition folders hangs its containerward chain: the
    member's containers as write-protected occurrences claiming nothing,
    under each of those its containers, breadth-first up to the roots or
    =max_roleTree_depth=; a container met again on the way is drawn once
    more as a leaf. Outside the diff view the chain passes through
    mirrors: the member and each chain node that is an occurrence with
    a fork partner the skgrepo restriction shows hold a =forksFolder=,
    as their first child, listing their mirrors write-protected, each
    with a folded =containerFolder= whose members carry their chains in
    turn; but not a note whose mirrors a =forksFolder= elsewhere in the
    view lists already (one in the buffer when the refresh began, or
    one it drew before), so that each set of mirrors is listed once and
    a cycle of containment and forks ends. The member's partners in the folder's own role are
    not drawn below it; a folder request on the member draws them.
    Nothing hangs under a ghost member. The refresh draws the chain
    under a member it draws and never reinstates a chain node, so a
    chain that stays keeps its headlines and their fold state, and a
    member the user deletes takes its chain with it. No request
    extends a chain; a =(folder containers)= request on a chain node
    draws a containers folder under it. A fresh content view puts the
    =containers= request on each root. Emitted by the =C-c l= commands.
    A member of a =forksFolder= is the exception outside the diff view:
    no chain hangs directly under it. Where the refresh draws one that
    is no repeat (an occurrence of a node another occurrence holds the
    editability of), it draws a =containerFolder= under it, as its
    first child (after an =integratedElsewhereFolder=, a
    =hiddenInForkPartnerFolder= and a =hiddenByForkerFolder=, if they
    are added later, before every other child), its members marked
    =folded=, so
    that only the folder's headline shows; each of its members carries
    the chain as above, and the folder, like any, lists no
    view-ancestor and is pruned when empty. A repeat gets nothing. A
    buffer drawn before this rule may hold chain nodes directly under
    such a member; the refresh reads them as its content (unwarranted,
    =noWarrant=, under a write-protected member). Reopening the view
    redraws it. A member of an =integratedElsewhereFolder= is drawn the
    same way: no chain directly under it, and a folded
    =containerFolder= under it, naming where the user keeps it.
  - =editableView= — on a write-protected occurrence, make it the node's
    editable one (the occurrence that was editable is write-protected,
    keeping its shown content, whose children claim nothing there); on
    an editable node, extend its content editable: each member it shows
    or draws whose node has no editable occurrence becomes editable,
    within =initial_node_limit= extensions, and passes the request on. A
    buffer carries at most one per node.
  - =writeProtectedView= — make the occurrence write-protected (its title
    resynced from the graph); its children then claim nothing, and keep
    their own editability and subtrees. The edits the occurrence carries
    in the same save are saved first. On a write-protected occurrence it
    does nothing. Emitted by =C-c s w=.
  - =writeProtectedSubtreeView= — the same for the occurrence and every
    occurrence of its content subtree. Emitted by =C-c s W=.
    Either write-protect request together with =editableView= on one
    occurrence is refused; on two occurrences of a node they move
    editability. The children of a write-protected occurrence carry no
    =noClaim=, so when it becomes editable again each claims, and
    those its content list does not name vanish: moving editability
    back restores the content.
  - =flags= — add one write-protected =flagsFolder=, retained even when empty.
    Its true-only viewnodes use =(flag hadId)=, =(flag wasOverloaded)=, and
    =(flag noSearchMatching)= metadata in that order. Viewnodes have no Org title;
    their friendly visible label comes from the herald. Deleting the entire
    folder dismisses this optional viewbranch and has no persistence meaning;
    edits within a retained folder are rejected rather than silently ignored.
  - =fork= — the explicit =skg-fork-node= gesture: clone this (owned)
    node into a private fork that forks it. Consumed on the save path
    at fork detection (the refresh leaves it alone), then dropped. Emitted
    by =skg-fork-node= (=C-c m f=).

  A ghost takes no view request; the parser refuses one there.
